Go made a language-design bet: failures should be visible in the code, not hidden in a throw path. So a Go function that can fail says so in its return values, and the caller's first order of business is checking:

```go
data, err := os.ReadFile(path)
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("load config: %w", err)
}
// data is valid here — no ambiguity
```

That guard is the entire error-handling mechanism. There's no catch block, no finally, no exception hierarchy to know — just a value check where the failure occurred. The pattern repeats at every layer, and each layer chooses: return it up (wrapped with context), handle it (retry, default, log), or crash (genuinely unrecoverable).

What the pattern buys is auditability: a reader can see every failure path of a function by reading the function — no stack of handlers elsewhere, no documentation of what throws. The cost is the visible ceremony — Go code has more if err != nil lines than exception code has catch keywords, and that verbosity is the most-criticized and most-defended property of the language.

The deeper point for an interview: this isn't boilerplate to minimize; it's control flow made literal. Every line of error handling is a decision the author made at a specific failure point — which is exactly what code review wants to see.

The convention — zero values on failure — is a contract between every function and every caller, and the classic bug is violating the READ ORDER of that contract. The canonical demonstration is buffered IO:

```go
n, err := r.Read(buf)
total := n             // BUG: captured before knowing if n is valid
if err != nil {
    return total, err  // returns a value the docs may not promise
}
```
On failure, n is 0 for a fresh reader — or, for some implementations, a partially-valid count that the docs say to use... or don't. The caller can't know which without reading the specific API. The convention resolves this: DON'T read other returns until err is nil, UNLESS the API explicitly documents partial results.

io.Reader's Read is the exception that defines the rule — 'n bytes were successfully read before the error' is a documented, universal behavior, and code that handles Read errors is EXPECTED to process buf[:n] first. That's why you see:

```go
for {
    n, err := r.Read(buf)
    total += n          // LEGAL here: Read documents n-before-err
    if err != nil {
        break           // with the data already counted
    }
}
```
The io.EOF convention (n>0 with err==io.EOF is legal, and the read loop processes the data THEN exits) makes streaming possible — every bufio and io.Copy depends on it. So the deep rule: check err first by default; read the API docs when the failure itself carries usable data; and io.Read/Write are the two families where partial-data-plus-error is contractual, not accidental.

Pass-through and wrap are both returns, with different information outcomes:

```go
// pass-through: caller sees the original error, unchanged
if err != nil {
    return err
}

// wrap: the caller sees what YOU were doing when it failed
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("load config %s: %w", path, err)
}
```

The pass-through is honest when your function adds nothing: a thin delegation where the caller already knows the operation. Wrapping is the default whenever your layer knows something the error doesn't carry — which file, which step, which request — because the caller's debugging question is always 'doing WHAT when this failed?'

The %w is what makes wrapping safe: it preserves the causal chain, so errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) still works through your added text. %v would embed the message but cut the chain — the caller could no longer match the underlying cause.

The anti-pattern to name: wrapping with no information. fmt.Errorf("failed: %w", err) adds five characters and zero meaning — every layer doing it stacks 'failed: failed: failed:' around the real message. Wrap when there's something to say; pass through when there isn't.
